{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Solgi","name":"Solgi, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parsa","familyName":"Madinei","name":"Madinei, Parsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupak","familyName":"Swaminathan","name":"Swaminathan, Rupak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Susanj","name":"Susanj, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation-Informed Pareto-Guided Low-Rank Compression for Efficient LLM/VLM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:07:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:21:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-03T18:20:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:06:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T20:51:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:13:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLM) and vision-language models (VLM) have achieved state-of-the-art performance, but they impose significant memory and computing challenges in deployment. We present a novel low-rank compression framework to address this challenge. First, we upper bound the change of network loss via layer-wise activation-based compression errors, filling a theoretical gap in the literature. We then formulate low-rank model compression as a bi-objective optimization and prove that a single uniform tolerance yields surrogate Pareto-optimal heterogeneous ranks. Based on our theoretical insights, we propose Pareto-Guided Singular Value Decomposition (PGSVD), a zero-shot pipeline that improves activation-aware compression via Pareto-guided rank selection and alternating least-squares implementation. We apply PGSVD to both LLM and VLM, showing better accuracy at the same compression levels and inference speedup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:20Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:03:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Forsyth","name":"Forsyth, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurabh","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Saurabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bimanual 3D Hand Motion and Articulation Forecasting in Everyday Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:18:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-19T17:20:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T00:47:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We tackle the problem of forecasting bimanual 3D hand motion and articulation from a single image in everyday settings. To address the lack of 3D hand annotations in diverse settings, we design an annotation pipeline consisting of a diffusion model to lift 2D hand keypoint sequences to 4D hand motion. For the forecasting model, we adopt a diffusion loss to account for the multimodality in hand motion distribution. Extensive experiments on 6 datasets show the benefits of training with our imputed labels (14% improvement) and the effectiveness of our lifting (45% better) \u0026amp; forecasting (16.4% gain) models, over the best baselines, especially in zero-shot generalization to everyday images."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://ap229997.github.io/projects/forehand4d"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:25Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:43:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lalithkumar","familyName":"Seenivasan","name":"Seenivasan, Lalithkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose L.","familyName":"Porras","name":"Porras, Jose L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger D.","familyName":"Soberanis-Mukul","name":"Soberanis-Mukul, Roger D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongchao","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Hongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin D.","familyName":"Killeen","name":"Killeen, Benjamin D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankita","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Ankita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lonny","familyName":"Yarmus","name":"Yarmus, Lonny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey K.","familyName":"Jopling","name":"Jopling, Jeffrey K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaru","familyName":"Ishii","name":"Ishii, Masaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela C.","familyName":"Argento","name":"Argento, Angela C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Unberath","name":"Unberath, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Egosurg: Arbitrary view synthesis for egocentric replay of operating room workflows from ambient cameras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:35:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:34:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-27T17:16:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-28T01:09:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Observing surgical practice has historically relied on fixed vantage points or recollections, leaving the egocentric perspectives that shape clinical decisions undocumented. Ambient fixed cameras capture the operating room (OR) at room scale but cannot recover what any individual team member actually saw. We present EgoSurg, a framework that reconstructs dynamic OR scenes from sparse wall-mounted stereo video and renders arbitrary, role-specific egocentric views without instrumenting personnel or interfering with clinical workflow. EgoSurg initializes a per-timestamp 3D Gaussian Splatting representation from scale-aware stereo depth and refines it with an image-conditioned diffusion model that corrects auxiliary rendered views, mitigating artifacts caused by limited camera coverage, crowding, and occlusion. We evaluated the framework on four real robotic pulmonology procedures and two simulated full-workflow sessions across two hospital sites. Near-field reconstruction fidelity was consistent (PSNR 26.8~dB, SSIM .895) across five workflow phases and both sites, and synthesized egocentric views reached a PSNR of 17.8~dB and an SSIM of .766 against paired hand-held point-of-view recordings. We further demonstrate three case studies for intended use: adjudicating a simulated sterile field violation, replaying a procedure from role-specific viewpoints, and testing a counterfactual change in personnel position. These results indicate that existing ambient camera infrastructure can be turned into a navigable 3D record of surgical work, supporting retrospective review of safety events, training, and workflow analysis from every angle."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:54:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:54:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-28T02:40:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinnosuke","familyName":"Saito","name":"Saito, Shinnosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Matsubara","name":"Matsubara, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Be Tangential to Manifold: Discovering Riemannian Metric for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:54:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:18:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-02T14:50:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-03T00:54:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-17T14:55:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-20T00:46:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models are powerful deep generative models, but unlike classical models, they lack an explicit low-dimensional latent space that parameterizes the data manifold. This absence makes it difficult to perform manifold-aware operations, such as geometrically faithful interpolation or conditional guidance that respects the learned manifold. We propose a training-free Riemannian metric on the noise space, derived from the Jacobian of the score function. The key insight is that the spectral structure of this Jacobian separates tangent and normal directions of the data manifold; our metric leverages this separation to encourage paths to stay tangential to the manifold rather than drift toward high-density regions. To validate that our metric faithfully captures the manifold geometry, we examine it from two complementary angles. First, geodesics under our metric yield perceptually more natural interpolations than existing methods on synthetic, image, and video frame datasets. Second, the tangent-normal decomposition induced by our metric prevents classifier-free guidance from deviating off the manifold, improving generation quality while preserving text-image alignment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-20T02:22:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhyeok","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Suhyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngsuk","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Youngsuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verifier-free Test-Time Sampling for Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-04T15:45:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) have demonstrated remarkable performance in robot control. However, they remain fundamentally limited in tasks that require high precision due to their single-inference paradigm. While test-time scaling approaches using external verifiers have shown promise, they require additional training and fail to generalize to unseen conditions. We propose Masking Distribution Guided Selection (MG-Select), a novel test-time scaling framework for VLAs that leverages the model's internal properties without requiring additional training or external modules. Our approach utilizes KL divergence from a reference action token distribution as a confidence metric for selecting the optimal action from multiple candidates. We introduce a reference distribution generated by the same VLA but with randomly masked states and language conditions as inputs, providing action uncertainty while remaining aligned with the target task distribution. Additionally, we propose a joint training strategy that enables the model to learn both conditional and unconditional distributions by applying dropout to state and language conditions, thereby further improving the quality of the reference distribution. Our experiments demonstrate that MG-Select provides a reliable reference for action selection through task-relevant condition masking and consistently improves base models across diverse simulation and real-world benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026. Project page: https://suhyeok-jang.github.io/mg-select/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:58:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05356","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05356","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05356","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostas","familyName":"Triaridis","name":"Triaridis, Kostas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandros","familyName":"Graikos","name":"Graikos, Alexandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aggelina","familyName":"Chatziagapi","name":"Chatziagapi, Aggelina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grigorios G.","familyName":"Chrysos","name":"Chrysos, Grigorios G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitris","familyName":"Samaras","name":"Samaras, Dimitris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Diffusion Model Hallucinations with Dynamic Guidance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:31:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:09:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-05T19:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T00:09:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hallucinations in diffusion models are samples with structural inconsistencies that can emerge due to the excessive smoothing of the learned score function, which in turn leads to interpolations between modes of the data distribution. Since semantic interpolations are often desirable and contribute to sample diversity, we believe that a nuanced and targeted solution is required to address diffusion model hallucinations. In this work, we introduce Dynamic Guidance, which mitigates hallucinations by selectively sharpening the score function only along the pre-determined directions known to cause artifacts, while preserving valid semantic variations. This sharpening can be performed using either pre-determined classes or semantically coherent clusters that form pseudo-classes over the data distribution. The latter allows for a principled extension of Dynamic Guidance to text-to-image generation, where we select modes to correspond to fine-grained contextual differences in textual descriptions. To our knowledge, this is the first approach that addresses hallucinations at generation time rather than through post-hoc filtering. Dynamic Guidance substantially reduces hallucinations on both controlled and natural image datasets, significantly outperforming baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://cvlab-stonybrook.github.io/DynamicGuidance/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05356","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:13:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05094","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05094","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05094","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gordon","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Gordon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Debevec","name":"Debevec, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VChain: Chain-of-Visual-Thought for Reasoning in Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T08:23:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent video generation models can produce smooth and visually appealing clips, but they often struggle to synthesize complex dynamics with a coherent chain of consequences. Accurately modeling visual outcomes and state transitions over time remains a core challenge. In contrast, large language and multimodal models (e.g., GPT-4o) exhibit strong visual state reasoning and future prediction capabilities. To bridge these strengths, we introduce VChain, a novel inference-time chain-of-visual-thought framework that injects visual reasoning signals from multimodal models into video generation. Specifically, VChain contains a dedicated pipeline that leverages large multimodal models to generate a sparse set of critical keyframes as snapshots, which are then used to guide the sparse inference-time visual-state adaptation of a pre-trained video generator only at these key moments. Our approach is tuning-efficient, introduces minimal overhead and avoids dense supervision. Extensive experiments on complex, multi-step scenarios show that VChain significantly enhances the quality of generated videos."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 (Findings Paper), ICCV 2025 Workshop Outstanding Paper Award, Project page: https://eyeline-labs.github.io/VChain"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05094","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:06:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:06:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:29:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05109","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05109","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05109","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijing","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinmiao","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xinmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tiny but Mighty: A Software-Hardware Co-Design Approach for Efficient Multimodal Inference on Battery-Powered Small Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T22:28:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T14:17:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:46:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T19:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:03:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T05:38:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:41:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-06T20:25:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:09:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-02T08:53:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:26:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) are inherently modular, comprising vision and audio encoders, a projector, and a language backbone. Yet existing systems execute them monolithically, underutilizing the heterogeneous accelerators (NPUs, GPUs, DSPs) on modern SoCs and inflating end-to-end latency. We present Nanomind, a hardware-software co-design inference framework that decomposes each LMM into modular \"bricks\"--vision, projector, language, and audio--and maps each brick to its best-suited compute units. A Token-Aware Buffer Manager (TABM) enables zero-copy embedding transfer across accelerators on unified-memory SoCs, bypassing CPU bottlenecks. Combined with customized hardware, a battery-aware scheduler, and fused low-bit GEMM kernels, Nanomind runs entirely on a compact, battery-powered prototype that operates fully offline. Nanomind reduces end-to-end energy by 42.3% against mainstream edge frameworks and devkits; in its on-demand low-power mode, the prototype runs LLaVA-OneVision-Qwen2-0.5B with a camera for nearly 18.8 hours on a single 2,000 mAh battery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05109","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:18Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry Jingchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Terry Jingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinya","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jixi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zirong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueling","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yueling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhe","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ruizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijing","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youpeng","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Youpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingqian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Bingqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhua","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jianhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Xiaodan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aligning Perception, Reasoning, Modeling and Interaction: A Survey on Physical AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:16:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T08:00:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:39:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-19T02:43:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:43:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-22T12:44:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-23T02:21:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-30T05:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:25:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of embodied intelligence and world models has intensified efforts to integrate physical laws into AI systems, yet physical perception and symbolic physics reasoning have developed along separate trajectories without a unified bridging framework. This work provides a comprehensive overview of physical AI, establishing clear distinctions between theoretical physics reasoning and applied physical understanding while systematically examining how physics-grounded methods enhance AI's real-world comprehension across structured symbolic reasoning, embodied systems, and generative models. Through rigorous analysis of recent advances, we advocate for intelligent systems that ground learning in both physical principles and embodied reasoning processes, transcending pattern recognition toward genuine understanding of physical laws. Our synthesis envisions next-generation world models capable of explaining physical phenomena and predicting future states, advancing safe, generalizable, and interpretable AI systems. We maintain a continuously updated resource at https://github.com/AI4Phys/Awesome-AI-for-Physics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:32:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuhe","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Jiuhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeju","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiange","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenyuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StaMo: Unsupervised Learning of Generalizable Robot Motion from Compact State Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:37:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T12:20:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:02:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A fundamental challenge in embodied intelligence is developing expressive and compact state representations for efficient world modeling and decision making. However, existing methods often fail to achieve this balance, yielding representations that are either overly redundant or lacking in task-critical information. We propose an unsupervised approach that learns a highly compressed two-token state representation using a lightweight encoder and a pre-trained Diffusion Transformer (DiT) decoder, capitalizing on its strong generative prior. Our representation is efficient, interpretable, and integrates seamlessly into existing VLA-based models, improving performance by 14.3% on LIBERO and 30% in real-world task success with minimal inference overhead. More importantly, we find that the difference between these tokens, obtained via latent interpolation, naturally serves as a highly effective latent action, which can be further decoded into executable robot actions. This emergent capability reveals that our representation captures structured dynamics without explicit supervision. We name our method StaMo for its ability to learn generalizable robotic Motion from compact State representation, which is encoded from static images, challenging the prevalent dependence to learning latent action on complex architectures and video data. The resulting latent actions also enhance policy co-training, outperforming prior methods by 10.4% with improved interpretability. Moreover, our approach scales effectively across diverse data sources, including real-world robot data, simulation, and human egocentric video."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Uzan","name":"Uzan, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asaf","familyName":"Yehudai","name":"Yehudai, Asaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roi","familyName":"pony","name":"pony, Roi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eyal","familyName":"Shnarch","name":"Shnarch, Eyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel","familyName":"Gera","name":"Gera, Ariel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guided Query Refinement: Multimodal Hybrid Retrieval with Test-Time Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:12:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T19:30:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T22:22:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:11:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal encoders have pushed the boundaries of visual document retrieval, matching textual query tokens directly to image patches and achieving state-of-the-art performance on public benchmarks. Recent models relying on this paradigm have massively scaled the sizes of their query and document representations, presenting obstacles to deployment and scalability in real-world pipelines. Furthermore, purely vision-centric approaches may be constrained by the inherent modality gap still exhibited by modern vision-language models. In this work, we connect these challenges to the paradigm of hybrid retrieval, investigating whether a lightweight dense text retriever can enhance a stronger vision-centric model. Existing hybrid methods, which rely on coarse-grained fusion of ranks or scores, fail to exploit the rich interactions within each model's representation space. To address this, we introduce Guided Query Refinement (GQR), a novel test-time optimization method that refines a primary retriever's query embedding using guidance from a complementary retriever's scores. Through extensive experiments on visual document retrieval benchmarks, we demonstrate that GQR allows vision-centric models to match the performance of models with significantly larger representations, while being up to 14x faster and requiring 54x less memory. Our findings show that GQR effectively pushes the Pareto frontier for performance and efficiency in multimodal retrieval. We release our code at https://github.com/IBM/test-time-hybrid-retrieval"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:31:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05725","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05725","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05725","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunsan","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Chunsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seonho","familyName":"An","name":"An, Seonho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min-Soo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Min-Soo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Discrete Diffusion Unmasking Policies Beyond Explicit Reference Policies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:37:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T06:25:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:27:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Masked diffusion models (MDMs) have recently emerged as a novel framework for language modeling. MDMs generate sentences by iteratively denoising masked sequences, filling in [MASK] tokens step by step. Although MDMs support any-order sampling, performance is highly sensitive to the choice of which position to unmask next. Prior work typically relies on rule-based schedules (e.g., max-confidence, max-margin), which provide ad hoc improvements. In contrast, we replace these heuristics with a learned scheduler. Specifically, we cast denoising as a KL-regularized Markov decision process (MDP) with an explicit reference policy and optimize a regularized objective that admits policy improvement and convergence guarantees under standard assumptions. We prove that the optimized policy under this framework generates samples that more closely match the data distribution than heuristic schedules. Empirically, across four benchmarks, our learned policy consistently outperforms max-confidence: for example, on SUDOKU, where unmasking order is critical, it yields a 20.1% gain over random and a 11.2% gain over max-confidence. Code is available at https://github.com/chunsanHong/UPO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05725","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05662","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05662","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05662","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyeop","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Taeyeop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyuree","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Gyuree","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Youngho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyeok","familyName":"Back","name":"Back, Seunghyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"In So","familyName":"Kweon","name":"Kweon, In So","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Hyunchul","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, David Hyunchul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuk-Jin","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Kuk-Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeLTa: Demonstration and Language-Guided Novel Transparent Object Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:18:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:32:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T00:07:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:12:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the prevalence of transparent object interactions in human everyday life, transparent robotic manipulation research remains limited to short-horizon tasks and basic grasping capabilities. Although some methods have partially addressed these issues, most of them have limitations in generalization to novel objects and are insufficient for precise long-horizon robot manipulation. To address this limitation, we propose DeLTa (Demonstration and Language-Guided Novel Transparent Object Manipulation), a novel framework that integrates depth estimation, 6D pose estimation, and vision-language planning for precise long-horizon manipulation of transparent objects guided by natural language task instructions. A key advantage of our method is its single-demonstration approach, which generalizes 6D trajectories to novel transparent objects without requiring category-level priors or additional training. Additionally, we present a task planner that refines the VLM-generated plan to account for the constraints of a single-arm, eye-in-hand robot for long-horizon object manipulation tasks. Through comprehensive evaluation, we demonstrate that our method significantly outperforms existing transparent object manipulation approaches, particularly in long-horizon scenarios requiring precise manipulation capabilities. Project page: https://sites.google.com/view/DeLTa25/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://sites.google.com/view/DeLTa25/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05662","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T03:09:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06050","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06050","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06050","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lüdke","name":"Lüdke, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marten","familyName":"Lienen","name":"Lienen, Marten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Kollovieh","name":"Kollovieh, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Günnemann","name":"Günnemann, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Edit-Based Flow Matching for Temporal Point Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:44:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T10:51:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T13:50:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:51:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Temporal point processes (TPPs) are a fundamental tool for modeling event sequences in continuous time, but most existing approaches rely on autoregressive parameterizations that are limited by their sequential sampling. Recent non-autoregressive, diffusion-style models mitigate these issues by jointly interpolating between noise and data through event insertions and deletions in a discrete Markov chain. In this work, we generalize this perspective and introduce an Edit Flow process for TPPs that transports noise to data via insert, delete, and substitute edit operations. By learning the instantaneous edit rates within a continuous-time Markov chain framework, we attain a flexible and efficient model that effectively reduces the total number of necessary edit operations during generation. Empirical results demonstrate the generative flexibility of our unconditionally trained model in a wide range of unconditional and conditional generation tasks on benchmark TPPs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06050","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-05T03:21:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05609","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05609","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05609","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peilin","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Peilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keiji","familyName":"Yanai","name":"Yanai, Keiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HOI-R1: Exploring the Potential of Multimodal Large Language Models for Human-Object Interaction Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T03:07:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:59:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent human-object interaction detection (HOID) methods highly require prior knowledge from vision-language models (VLMs) to enhance the interaction recognition capabilities. The training strategies and model architectures for connecting the knowledge from VLMs to the HOI instance representations from the object detector are challenging, and the whole framework is complex for further development or application. On the other hand, the inherent reasoning abilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) on human-object interaction detection are under-explored. Inspired by the recent success of training MLLMs with reinforcement learning (RL) methods, we propose HOI-R1 and first explore the potential of the language model on the HOID task without any additional detection modules. We introduce an HOI reasoning process and HOID reward functions to solve the HOID task by pure text. Experiments on HICO-DET across multiple open-source MLLMs, including the Qwen-VL family (Qwen2.5-VL and Qwen3-VL) and Rex-Omni, show consistent improvements. Especially, HOI-R1 boosts Qwen2.5-VL-3B 2$\\times$ accuracy with great generalization ability. The source code is available at https://github.com/cjw2021/HOI-R1."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05609","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05580","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05580","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05580","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhantao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Fangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anudeepsekhar","familyName":"Bolimera","name":"Bolimera, Anudeepsekhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marios","familyName":"Savvides","name":"Savvides, Marios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaVLA: Unified Meta Co-training For Efficient Embodied Adaption"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:54:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T08:17:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T01:37:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T03:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:19:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models show promise in embodied reasoning, yet remain far from true generalists-they often require task-specific fine-tuning, incur high compute costs, and generalize poorly to unseen tasks. We propose MetaVLA, a unified, backbone-agnostic post-training framework for efficient and scalable alignment. MetaVLA introduces Context-Aware Meta Co-Training, which consolidates diverse target tasks into a single fine-tuning stage while leveraging structurally diverse auxiliary tasks to improve in-domain generalization. Unlike naive multi-task SFT, MetaVLA integrates a lightweight meta-learning mechanism-derived from Attentive Neural Processes-to enable rapid adaptation from diverse contexts with minimal architectural change or inference overhead. On the LIBERO benchmark, MetaVLA with six auxiliary tasks outperforms OpenVLA by up to 8.0% on long-horizon tasks, reduces training steps from 240K to 75K, and cuts GPU time by ~76%. These results show that scalable, low-resource post-training is achievable-paving the way toward general-purpose embodied agents. Code will be available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05580","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:11Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-29T03:13:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06215","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06215","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06215","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayush","familyName":"Shrivastava","name":"Shrivastava, Ayush","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Connelly","familyName":"Barnes","name":"Barnes, Connelly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuaner","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuaner","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Owens","name":"Owens, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sohrab","familyName":"Amirghodsi","name":"Amirghodsi, Sohrab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eli","familyName":"Shechtman","name":"Shechtman, Eli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-grained Defocus Blur Control for Generative Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-12T05:39:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:23:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current text-to-image diffusion models excel at generating diverse, high-quality images, yet they struggle to incorporate fine-grained camera metadata such as precise aperture settings. In this work, we introduce a novel text-to-image diffusion framework that leverages camera metadata, or EXIF data, which is often embedded in image files, with an emphasis on generating controllable lens blur. Our method mimics the physical image formation process by first generating an all-in-focus image, estimating its monocular depth, predicting a plausible focus distance with a novel focus distance transformer, and then forming a defocused image with an existing differentiable lens blur model. Gradients flow backwards through this whole process, allowing us to learn without explicit supervision to generate defocus effects based on content elements and the provided EXIF data. At inference time, this enables precise interactive user control over defocus effects while preserving scene contents, which is not achievable with existing diffusion models. Experimental results demonstrate that our model enables superior fine-grained control without altering the depicted scene."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project link: https://www.ayshrv.com/defocus-blur-gen"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06215","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-15T02:58:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yolo Y.","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yolo Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Bi","name":"Bi, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinxin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhangyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianxiang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiani","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yunzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Susan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Susan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhua","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Xing","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jia-Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bozheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bozheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Daiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyun","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Ziyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Vosoughi","name":"Vosoughi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luchuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Luchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiki","familyName":"Shimada","name":"Shimada, Daiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiebo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jiebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenliang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Video-LMM Post-Training: A Deep Dive into Video Reasoning with Large Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:10:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T16:08:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:59:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:15:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T16:09:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:07:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-28T18:02:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:03:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-25T03:36:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:24:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video understanding represents the most challenging frontier in computer vision, requiring models to reason about complex spatiotemporal relationships, long-term dependencies, and multimodal evidence. The recent emergence of Video-Large Multimodal Models (Video-LMMs), which integrate visual encoders with powerful decoder-based language models, has demonstrated remarkable capabilities in video understanding tasks. However, the critical phase that transforms these models from basic perception systems into sophisticated reasoning engines, post-training, remains fragmented across the literature. This survey provides the first comprehensive examination of post-training methodologies for Video-LMMs, encompassing three fundamental pillars: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with chain-of-thought, reinforcement learning (RL) from verifiable objectives, and test-time scaling (TTS) through enhanced inference computation. We present a structured taxonomy that clarifies the roles, interconnections, and video-specific adaptations of these techniques, addressing unique challenges such as temporal localization, spatiotemporal grounding, long video efficiency, and multimodal evidence integration. Through systematic analysis of representative methods, we synthesize key design principles, insights, and evaluation protocols while identifying critical open challenges in reward design, scalability, and cost-performance optimization. We further curate essential benchmarks, datasets, and metrics to facilitate rigorous assessment of post-training effectiveness. This survey aims to provide researchers and practitioners with a unified framework for advancing Video-LMM capabilities. Additional resources and updates are maintained at: https://github.com/yunlong10/Awesome-Video-LMM-Post-Training"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Version v1.1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-26T03:24:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Cai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Wipf","name":"Wipf, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Powerful Ways to Generate: Autoregression, Diffusion, and Beyond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T03:58:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:22:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models have recently emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive language models. Beyond next-token generation, they are more efficient and flexible by enabling parallel and any-order token generation. However, despite empirical successes, their computational power and fundamental limitations remain poorly understood. In this paper, we formally study whether non-autoregressive generation in Masked Diffusion Models (MDM) enables solving problems beyond the reach of Auto-Regressive Models (ARM). Our results show that MDM with sufficiently large context length is computationally universal with decoding steps matching the optimal parallel time complexity in PRAM. However, when controlling for other factors, MDM's flexibility to generate in any-order does not expand what ARM can already solve. To address this, we propose a new form of generation called any-process generation, which extends MDM with capabilities to remask, insert and delete tokens, allowing self-correction, length-variable editing, and adaptive parallelism. Theoretically and empirically, we demonstrate these capabilities enable scalability to significantly harder reasoning problems that are otherwise intractable for ARM and vanilla MDM. Additionally, they prove essential for generation tasks where objects naturally evolve through non-sequential processes, crucial for extending current LLMs beyond natural language to domains such as coding and science."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Python","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Python","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke Tenyi","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Luke Tenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Yun","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Yun-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Penghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Penghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning under Vision: Understanding Visual-Spatial Cognition in Vision-Language Models for CAPTCHA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:56:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-16T00:34:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:48:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"CAPTCHA, originally designed to distinguish humans from robots, has evolved into a real-world benchmark for assessing the spatial reasoning capabilities of vision-language models. In this work, we first show that step-by-step reasoning is crucial for vision-language models (VLMs) to solve CAPTCHAs, which represent high-difficulty spatial reasoning tasks, and that current commercial vision-language models still struggle with such reasoning. In particular, we observe that most commercial VLMs (e.g., Gemini, Claude, GPT, etc.) fail to effectively solve CAPTCHAs and thus achieve low accuracy (around 21.9 percent). However, our findings indicate that requiring the model to perform step-by-step reasoning before generating the final coordinates can significantly enhance its solving accuracy, underscoring the severity of the gap. To systematically study this issue, we introduce CAPTCHA-X, the first real-world CAPTCHA benchmark with reasoning, covering seven categories of CAPTCHAs (such as Gobang, hCaptcha, etc.) with step-by-step action solutions and grounding annotations. We further define five reasoning-oriented metrics that enable a comprehensive evaluation of models reasoning capabilities. To validate the effectiveness of reasoning, we also propose a general agentic VLM-based framework that incorporates the models inherent reasoning abilities. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance across five high-difficulty CAPTCHA types, with an average solving accuracy of 83.9 percent, substantially surpassing existing baselines. These results reveal the limitations of current models and highlight the importance of reasoning in advancing visual-spatial challenges in the future."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14pages, 11figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zefu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zefu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongxu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Rongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Hanning","name":"Hanning, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjia","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Junjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojuan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiaojuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wenbo","name":"Wenbo, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lue","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenling","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EmbodiedCoder: Parameterized Embodied Mobile Manipulation via Modern Coding Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T01:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:16:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in control robot methods, from end-to-end vision-language-action frameworks to modular systems with predefined primitives, have advanced robots' ability to follow natural language instructions. Nonetheless, many approaches still struggle to scale to diverse environments, as they often rely on large annotated datasets and offer limited interpretability.In this work, we introduce EmbodiedCoder, a training-free framework for open-world mobile robot manipulation that leverages coding models to directly generate executable robot trajectories. By grounding high-level instructions in code, EmbodiedCoder enables flexible object geometry parameterization and manipulation trajectory synthesis without additional data collection or fine-tuning.This coding-based paradigm provides a transparent and generalizable way to connect perception with manipulation. Experiments on real mobile robots show that EmbodiedCoder achieves robust performance across diverse long-term tasks and generalizes effectively to novel objects and environments.Our results demonstrate an interpretable approach for bridging high-level reasoning and low-level control, moving beyond fixed primitives toward versatile robot intelligence. See the project page at: https://embodiedcoder.github.io/EmbodiedCoder/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Demo Page: https://embodiedcoder.github.io/EmbodiedCoder/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:53Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peigui","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Peigui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunsheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Kunsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenghai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Nenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SafeGuider: Robust and Practical Content Safety Control for Text-to-Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:24:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:00:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:22:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:53:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:36:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3719027.3744835","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image models have shown remarkable capabilities in generating high-quality images from natural language descriptions. However, these models are highly vulnerable to adversarial prompts, which can bypass safety measures and produce harmful content. Despite various defensive strategies, achieving robustness against attacks while maintaining practical utility in real-world applications remains a significant challenge. To address this issue, we first conduct an empirical study of the text encoder in the Stable Diffusion (SD) model, which is a widely used and representative text-to-image model. Our findings reveal that the [EOS] token acts as a semantic aggregator, exhibiting distinct distributional patterns between benign and adversarial prompts in its embedding space. Building on this insight, we introduce SafeGuider, a two-step framework designed for robust safety control without compromising generation quality. SafeGuider combines an embedding-level recognition model with a safety-aware feature erasure beam search algorithm. This integration enables the framework to maintain high-quality image generation for benign prompts while ensuring robust defense against both in-domain and out-of-domain attacks. SafeGuider demonstrates exceptional effectiveness in minimizing attack success rates, achieving a maximum rate of only 5.48\\% across various attack scenarios. Moreover, instead of refusing to generate or producing black images for unsafe prompts, SafeGuider generates safe and meaningful images, enhancing its practical utility. In addition, SafeGuider is not limited to the SD model and can be effectively applied to other text-to-image models, such as the Flux model, demonstrating its versatility and adaptability across different architectures. We hope that SafeGuider can shed some light on the practical deployment of secure text-to-image systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM CCS 2025, Code is available at [this https URL](https://github.com/pgqihere/safeguider)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Kondo","name":"Kondo, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. P.","familyName":"How","name":"How, J. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Navigation in Unknown Indoor Environments with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:26:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T16:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel high-level planning framework that leverages vision-language models (VLMs) to improve autonomous navigation in unknown indoor environments with many dead ends. Traditional exploration methods often take inefficient routes due to limited global reasoning and reliance on local heuristics. In contrast, our approach enables a VLM to reason directly about occupancy maps in a zero-shot manner, selecting subgoals that are likely to yield more efficient paths. At each planning step, we convert a 3D occupancy grid into a partial 2D map of the environment, and generate candidate subgoals. Each subgoal is then evaluated and ranked against other candidates by the model. We integrate this planning scheme into DYNUS \\cite{kondo2025dynus}, a state-of-the-art trajectory planner, and demonstrate improved navigation efficiency in simulation. The VLM infers structural patterns (e.g., rooms, corridors) from incomplete maps and balances the need to make progress toward a goal against the risk of entering unknown space. This reduces common greedy failures (e.g., detouring into small rooms) and achieves about 10\\% shorter paths on average."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures, accepted to the OWN workshop at IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06080","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06080","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06080","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nga","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Nga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Bonnefon","name":"Bonnefon, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"René","familyName":"Gato","name":"Gato, René","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Almeida","name":"Almeida, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel","familyName":"Roques","name":"Roques, Lionel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanistic-statistical inference of mosquito dynamics from mark-release-recapture data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Populations and Evolution (q-bio.PE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"92D25, 60J60, 35K57, 62P10, 62F10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:06:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T19:58:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:08:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Biological control strategies against mosquito-borne diseases--such as the sterile insect technique (SIT), RIDL, and Wolbachia-based releases--require reliable estimates of dispersal and survival of released males. We propose a mechanistic--statistical framework for mark--release--recapture (MRR) data linking an individual-based 2D diffusion model with its reaction--diffusion limit. Inference is based on solving the macroscopic system and embedding it in a Poisson observation model for daily trap counts, with uncertainty quantified via a parametric bootstrap. We validate identifiability using simulated data and apply the model to an urban MRR campaign in El Cano (Havana, Cuba) involving four weekly releases of sterile Aedes aegypti males. The best-supported model suggests a mean life expectancy of about five days and a typical displacement of about 180 m. Unlike empirical fits of survival or dispersal, our mechanistic approach jointly estimates movement, mortality, and capture, yielding biologically interpretable parameters and a principled framework for designing and evaluating SIT-based interventions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06080","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06209","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06209","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06209","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenpei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhenpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijing","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuliang","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijit","familyName":"Kundu","name":"Kundu, Abhijit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Lezama","name":"Lezama, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luna Yue","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Luna Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jyh-Jing","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Jyh-Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dragomir","familyName":"Anguelov","name":"Anguelov, Dragomir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chiyu Max","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chiyu Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Drive\u0026amp;Gen: Co-Evaluating End-to-End Driving and Video Generation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in generative models have sparked exciting new possibilities in the field of autonomous vehicles. Specifically, video generation models are now being explored as controllable virtual testing environments. Simultaneously, end-to-end (E2E) driving models have emerged as a streamlined alternative to conventional modular autonomous driving systems, gaining popularity for their simplicity and scalability. However, the application of these techniques to simulation and planning raises important questions. First, while video generation models can generate increasingly realistic videos, can these videos faithfully adhere to the specified conditions and be realistic enough for E2E autonomous planner evaluation? Second, given that data is crucial for understanding and controlling E2E planners, how can we gain deeper insights into their biases and improve their ability to generalize to out-of-distribution scenarios? In this work, we bridge the gap between the driving models and generative world models (Drive\u0026amp;Gen) to address these questions. We propose novel statistical measures leveraging E2E drivers to evaluate the realism of generated videos. By exploiting the controllability of the video generation model, we conduct targeted experiments to investigate distribution gaps affecting E2E planner performance. Finally, we show that synthetic data produced by the video generation model offers a cost-effective alternative to real-world data collection. This synthetic data effectively improves E2E model generalization beyond existing Operational Design Domains, facilitating the expansion of autonomous vehicle services into new operational contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06209","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan X.","familyName":"Kodama","name":"Kodama, Nathan X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Hinczewski","name":"Hinczewski, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thermodynamic Performance Limits for Score-Based Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a fundamental connection between score-based diffusion models and non-equilibrium thermodynamics by deriving performance limits based on entropy rates. Our main theoretical contribution is a lower bound on the negative log-likelihood of the data that relates model performance to entropy rates of diffusion processes. We numerically validate this bound on a synthetic dataset and investigate its tightness. By building a bridge to entropy rates - system, intrinsic, and exchange entropy - we provide new insights into the thermodynamic operation of these models, drawing parallels to Maxwell's demon and implications for thermodynamic computing hardware. Our framework connects generative modeling performance to fundamental physical principles through stochastic thermodynamics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangqiong","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Liangqiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daguang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Daguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrete Diffusion Models with MLLMs for Unified Medical Multimodal Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:06:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in generative medical models are constrained by modality-specific scenarios that hinder the integration of complementary evidence from imaging, pathology, and clinical notes. This fragmentation limits their evolution into foundation models that can learn and reason across the full spectrum of biomedical data. We propose MeDiM, the first medical discrete diffusion model that learns shared distributions across modalities without modality-specific components. MeDiM unifies multiple generative tasks: translating between images and text, and jointly producing image-report pairs across domains in response to prompts. Built on a discrete diffusion framework, MeDiM bridges vision and language representations through a shared probabilistic space. To enable unified and flexible medical generation, we employ a multimodal large language model (MLLM) as the diffusion backbone, leveraging its prior knowledge and cross-modal reasoning. Two key designs are introduced: (1) removing the causal attention mask for bidirectional context, and (2) injecting continuous timestep embeddings for diffusion awareness. Experiments demonstrate high-fidelity medical generation (FID 16.60 on MIMIC-CXR and FID 24.19 on PathGen) and accurate report generation (METEOR 0.2650 and 0.2580). Jointly generated image-report pairs further enhance downstream performance (plus6.43 percent BLEU-1, plus18.57 percent BLEU-2, plus31.58 percent BLEU-3, plus4.80 percent METEOR), showing that MeDiM supports coherent and clinically grounded multimodal outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages,6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Mi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihui","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Zihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Dimakis","name":"Dimakis, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristen","familyName":"Grauman","name":"Grauman, Kristen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Thinking Drifts: Evidential Grounding for Robust Video Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:03:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video reasoning, the task of enabling machines to infer from dynamic visual content through multi-step logic, is crucial for advanced AI. While the Chain-of-Thought (CoT) mechanism has enhanced reasoning in text-based tasks, its application to video understanding remains underexplored. This paper presents a systematic analysis revealing that CoT often degrades performance in video reasoning, generating verbose but misleading internal monologues, and leading to hallucinated visual details and overridden correct intuitions - a phenomenon we term \"visual thinking drift\". We explain this drift through a Bayesian lens, positing that CoT traces often diverge from actual visual evidence, instead amplifying internal biases or language priors, causing models to storytell rather than engage in grounded reasoning. To counteract this, we introduce Visual Evidence Reward (VER), a novel reinforcement learning framework that explicitly rewards the generation of reasoning traces that are verifiably grounded in visual evidence. Comprehensive evaluation across 10 diverse video understanding benchmarks demonstrates that our Video-VER consistently achieves top performance. Our work sheds light on the distinct challenges of video-centric reasoning and encourages the development of AI that robustly grounds its inferences in visual evidence - for large multimodal models that not only \"think before answering\", but also \"see while thinking\"."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025, Project page: https://vision.cs.utexas.edu/projects/video-ver/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Muppidi","name":"Muppidi, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Radfar","name":"Radfar, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Medical Vision Language Models as Policies for Robotic Surgery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/cai64502.2025.00094","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-based Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) struggles with visual observation-based robotic laparoscopic surgical tasks due to the high-dimensional nature of visual input, the sparsity of rewards in surgical environments, and the difficulty of extracting task-relevant features from raw visual data. We introduce a simple approach integrating MedFlamingo, a medical domain-specific Vision-Language Model, with PPO. Our method is evaluated on five diverse laparoscopic surgery task environments in LapGym, using only endoscopic visual observations. MedFlamingo PPO outperforms and converges faster compared to both standard vision-based PPO and OpenFlamingo PPO baselines, achieving task success rates exceeding 70% across all environments, with improvements ranging from 66.67% to 1114.29% compared to baseline. By processing task observations and instructions once per episode to generate high-level planning tokens, our method efficiently combines medical expertise with real-time visual feedback. Our results highlight the value of specialized medical knowledge in robotic surgical planning and decision-making."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE CAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Marinoni","name":"Marinoni, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo Fosco","familyName":"Gramaccioni","name":"Gramaccioni, Riccardo Fosco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eleonora","familyName":"Grassucci","name":"Grassucci, Eleonora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danilo","familyName":"Comminiello","name":"Comminiello, Danilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Controllable Audio-Visual Viewpoint Generation from 360° Spatial Information"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:53:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The generation of sounding videos has seen significant advancements with the advent of diffusion models. However, existing methods often lack the fine-grained control needed to generate viewpoint-specific content from larger, immersive 360-degree environments. This limitation restricts the creation of audio-visual experiences that are aware of off-camera events. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to introduce a framework for controllable audio-visual generation, addressing this unexplored gap. Specifically, we propose a diffusion model by introducing a set of powerful conditioning signals derived from the full 360-degree space: a panoramic saliency map to identify regions of interest, a bounding-box-aware signed distance map to define the target viewpoint, and a descriptive caption of the entire scene. By integrating these controls, our model generates spatially-aware viewpoint videos and audios that are coherently influenced by the broader, unseen environmental context, introducing a strong controllability that is essential for realistic and immersive audio-visual generation. We show audiovisual examples proving the effectiveness of our framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinfeng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liejun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuchun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Fuchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ECTSpeech: Enhancing Efficient Speech Synthesis via Easy Consistency Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:44:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have demonstrated remarkable performance in speech synthesis, but typically require multi-step sampling, resulting in low inference efficiency. Recent studies address this issue by distilling diffusion models into consistency models, enabling efficient one-step generation. However, these approaches introduce additional training costs and rely heavily on the performance of pre-trained teacher models. In this paper, we propose ECTSpeech, a simple and effective one-step speech synthesis framework that, for the first time, incorporates the Easy Consistency Tuning (ECT) strategy into speech synthesis. By progressively tightening consistency constraints on a pre-trained diffusion model, ECTSpeech achieves high-quality one-step generation while significantly reducing training complexity. In addition, we design a multi-scale gate module (MSGate) to enhance the denoiser's ability to fuse features at different scales. Experimental results on the LJSpeech dataset demonstrate that ECTSpeech achieves audio quality comparable to state-of-the-art methods under single-step sampling, while substantially reducing the model's training cost and complexity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication by Proceedings of the 2025 ACM Multimedia Asia Conference(MMAsia '25)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:39Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunyi","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Yunyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Finn","familyName":"Carter","name":"Carter, Finn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion-Based Image Editing for Breaking Robust Watermarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:34:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robust invisible watermarking aims to embed hidden information into images such that the watermark can survive various image manipulations. However, the rise of powerful diffusion-based image generation and editing techniques poses a new threat to these watermarking schemes. In this paper, we present a theoretical study and method demonstrating that diffusion models can effectively break robust image watermarks that were designed to resist conventional perturbations. We show that a diffusion-driven ``image regeneration'' process can erase embedded watermarks while preserving perceptual image content. We further introduce a novel guided diffusion attack that explicitly targets the watermark signal during generation, significantly degrading watermark detectability. Theoretically, we prove that as an image undergoes sufficient diffusion-based transformation, the mutual information between the watermarked image and the embedded watermark payload vanishes, resulting in decoding failure. Experimentally, we evaluate our approach on multiple state-of-the-art watermarking schemes (including the deep learning-based methods StegaStamp, TrustMark, and VINE) and demonstrate near-zero watermark recovery rates after attack, while maintaining high visual fidelity of the regenerated images. Our findings highlight a fundamental vulnerability in current robust watermarking techniques against generative model-based attacks, underscoring the need for new watermarking strategies in the era of generative AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eashan","familyName":"Adhikarla","name":"Adhikarla, Eashan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian D.","familyName":"Davison","name":"Davison, Brian D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion Models for Low-Light Image Enhancement: A Multi-Perspective Taxonomy and Performance Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-light image enhancement (LLIE) is vital for safety-critical applications such as surveillance, autonomous navigation, and medical imaging, where visibility degradation can impair downstream task performance. Recently, diffusion models have emerged as a promising generative paradigm for LLIE due to their capacity to model complex image distributions via iterative denoising. This survey provides an up-to-date critical analysis of diffusion models for LLIE, distinctively featuring an in-depth comparative performance evaluation against Generative Adversarial Network and Transformer-based state-of-the-art methods, a thorough examination of practical deployment challenges, and a forward-looking perspective on the role of emerging paradigms like foundation models. We propose a multi-perspective taxonomy encompassing six categories: Intrinsic Decomposition, Spectral \u0026amp; Latent, Accelerated, Guided, Multimodal, and Autonomous; that map enhancement methods across physical priors, conditioning schemes, and computational efficiency. Our taxonomy is grounded in a hybrid view of both the model mechanism and the conditioning signals. We evaluate qualitative failure modes, benchmark inconsistencies, and trade-offs between interpretability, generalization, and inference efficiency. We also discuss real-world deployment constraints (e.g., memory, energy use) and ethical considerations. This survey aims to guide the next generation of diffusion-based LLIE research by highlighting trends and surfacing open research questions, including novel conditioning, real-time adaptation, and the potential of foundation models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05949","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05949","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05949","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Randall","familyName":"Balestriero","name":"Balestriero, Randall","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Ballas","name":"Ballas, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Rabbat","name":"Rabbat, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yann","familyName":"LeCun","name":"LeCun, Yann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gaussian Embeddings: How JEPAs Secretly Learn Your Data Density"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:06:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:50:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Joint Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) learn representations able to solve numerous downstream tasks out-of-the-box. JEPAs combine two objectives: (i) a latent-space prediction term, i.e., the representation of a slightly perturbed sample must be predictable from the original sample's representation, and (ii) an anti-collapse term, i.e., not all samples should have the same representation. While (ii) is often considered as an obvious remedy to representation collapse, we uncover that JEPAs' anti-collapse term does much more--it provably estimates the data density. In short, any successfully trained JEPA can be used to get sample probabilities, e.g., for data curation, outlier detection, or simply for density estimation. Our theoretical finding is agnostic of the dataset and architecture used--in any case one can compute the learned probabilities of sample $x$ efficiently and in closed-form using the model's Jacobian matrix at $x$. Our findings are empirically validated across datasets (synthetic, controlled, and Imagenet) and across different Self Supervised Learning methods falling under the JEPA family (I-JEPA and DINOv2) and on multimodal models, such as MetaCLIP. We denote the method extracting the JEPA learned density as {\\bf JEPA-SCORE}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05949","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:00:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongzhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aradhana","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Aradhana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunli","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qunli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo","familyName":"Cangelosi","name":"Cangelosi, Angelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Safety Challenge of World Models for Embodied AI Agents: A Review"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:35:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid progress in embodied artificial intelligence has highlighted the necessity for more advanced and integrated models that can perceive, interpret, and predict environmental dynamics. In this context, World Models (WMs) have been introduced to provide embodied agents with the abilities to anticipate future environmental states and fill in knowledge gaps, thereby enhancing agents' ability to plan and execute actions. However, when dealing with embodied agents it is fundamental to ensure that predictions are safe for both the agent and the environment. In this article, we conduct a comprehensive literature review of World Models in the domains of autonomous driving and robotics, with a specific focus on the safety implications of scene and control generation tasks. Our review is complemented by an empirical analysis, wherein we collect and examine predictions from state-of-the-art models, identify and categorize common faults (herein referred to as pathologies), and provide a quantitative evaluation of the results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05722","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05722","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05722","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaojiao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jiaojiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niki","familyName":"Trigoni","name":"Trigoni, Niki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Markham","name":"Markham, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data Factory with Minimal Human Effort Using VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:43:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:37:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating enough and diverse data through augmentation offers an efficient solution to the time-consuming and labour-intensive process of collecting and annotating pixel-wise images. Traditional data augmentation techniques often face challenges in manipulating high-level semantic attributes, such as materials and textures. In contrast, diffusion models offer a robust alternative, by effectively utilizing text-to-image or image-to-image transformation. However, existing diffusion-based methods are either computationally expensive or compromise on performance. To address this issue, we introduce a novel training-free pipeline that integrates pretrained ControlNet and Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to generate synthetic images paired with pixel-level labels. This approach eliminates the need for manual annotations and significantly improves downstream tasks. To improve the fidelity and diversity, we add a Multi-way Prompt Generator, Mask Generator and High-quality Image Selection module. Our results on PASCAL-5i and COCO-20i present promising performance and outperform concurrent work for one-shot semantic segmentation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Tech report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05722","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:55:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hedi","familyName":"Zisling","name":"Zisling, Hedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilan","familyName":"Naiman","name":"Naiman, Ilan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nimrod","familyName":"Berman","name":"Berman, Nimrod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Supasorn","familyName":"Suwajanakorn","name":"Suwajanakorn, Supasorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Azencot","name":"Azencot, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:30:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised representation learning, particularly sequential disentanglement, aims to separate static and dynamic factors of variation in data without relying on labels. This remains a challenging problem, as existing approaches based on variational autoencoders and generative adversarial networks often rely on multiple loss terms, complicating the optimization process. Furthermore, sequential disentanglement methods face challenges when applied to real-world data, and there is currently no established evaluation protocol for assessing their performance in such settings. Recently, diffusion models have emerged as state-of-the-art generative models, but no theoretical formalization exists for their application to sequential disentanglement. In this work, we introduce the Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Autoencoder (DiffSDA), a novel, modal-agnostic framework effective across diverse real-world data modalities, including time series, video, and audio. DiffSDA leverages a new probabilistic modeling, latent diffusion, and efficient samplers, while incorporating a challenging evaluation protocol for rigorous testing. Our experiments on diverse real-world benchmarks demonstrate that DiffSDA outperforms recent state-of-the-art methods in sequential disentanglement."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:55:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziheng","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Ziheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng-Tao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Peng-Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibin","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Qibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgeBooth: Controllable Facial Aging and Rejuvenation via Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:25:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:35:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent diffusion model research focuses on generating identity-consistent images from a reference photo, but they struggle to accurately control age while preserving identity, and fine-tuning such models often requires costly paired images across ages. In this paper, we propose AgeBooth, a novel age-specific finetuning approach that can effectively enhance the age control capability of adapterbased identity personalization models without the need for expensive age-varied datasets. To reduce dependence on a large amount of age-labeled data, we exploit the linear nature of aging by introducing age-conditioned prompt blending and an age-specific LoRA fusion strategy that leverages SVDMix, a matrix fusion technique. These techniques enable high-quality generation of intermediate-age portraits. Our AgeBooth produces realistic and identity-consistent face images across different ages from a single reference image. Experiments show that AgeBooth achieves superior age control and visual quality compared to previous state-of-the-art editing-based methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:19Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:55:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05689","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05689","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05689","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayub","familyName":"Ahmadi","name":"Ahmadi, Ayub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdieh","familyName":"Tahmasebi","name":"Tahmasebi, Mahdieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hedging of exotic options in Hawkes jump-diffusion models by Malliavin calculus"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60H07, 91G20, 65c30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:47:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In financial mathematics, the calculation of the Greeks, especially the delta, is emphasized due to its role in risk management. In this article, we employ Malliavin calculus to determine the delta of European and Asian options, where the underlying asset evolves according to a Hawkes jump-diffusion process. A central feature is that the Hawkes jump intensity is stochastic, which substantially affects the delta representation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2 tables and 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05689","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:42Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:54:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05660","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05660","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05660","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K J","familyName":"Joseph","name":"Joseph, K J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"De La Torre","name":"De La Torre, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teleportraits: Training-Free People Insertion into Any Scene"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:12:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:31:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The task of realistically inserting a human from a reference image into a background scene is highly challenging, requiring the model to (1) determine the correct location and poses of the person and (2) perform high-quality personalization conditioned on the background. Previous approaches often treat them as separate problems, overlooking their interconnections, and typically rely on training to achieve high performance. In this work, we introduce a unified training-free pipeline that leverages pre-trained text-to-image diffusion models. We show that diffusion models inherently possess the knowledge to place people in complex scenes without requiring task-specific training. By combining inversion techniques with classifier-free guidance, our method achieves affordance-aware global editing, seamlessly inserting people into scenes. Furthermore, our proposed mask-guided self-attention mechanism ensures high-quality personalization, preserving the subject's identity, clothing, and body features from just a single reference image. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to perform realistic human insertions into scenes in a training-free manner and achieve state-of-the-art results in diverse composite scene images with excellent identity preservation in backgrounds and subjects."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05660","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:54:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Conditional Generation on Scale-based Visual Autoregressive Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:27:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in autoregressive (AR) models have demonstrated their potential to rival diffusion models in image synthesis. However, for complex spatially-conditioned generation, current AR approaches rely on fine-tuning the pre-trained model, leading to significant training costs. In this paper, we propose the Efficient Control Model (ECM), a plug-and-play framework featuring a lightweight control module that introduces control signals via a distributed architecture. This architecture consists of context-aware attention layers that refine conditional features using real-time generated tokens, and a shared gated feed-forward network (FFN) designed to maximize the utilization of its limited capacity and ensure coherent control feature learning. Furthermore, recognizing the critical role of early-stage generation in determining semantic structure, we introduce an early-centric sampling strategy that prioritizes learning early control sequences. This approach reduces computational cost by lowering the number of training tokens per iteration, while a complementary temperature scheduling during inference compensates for the resulting insufficient training of late-stage tokens. Extensive experiments on scale-based AR models validate that our method achieves high-fidelity and diverse control over image generation, surpassing existing baselines while significantly improving both training and inference efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:53Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:52:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Sartor","name":"Sartor, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pieter","familyName":"Peers","name":"Peers, Pieter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teamwork: Collaborative Diffusion with Low-rank Coordination and Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:44:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3757377.3763870","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large pretrained diffusion models can provide strong priors beneficial for many graphics applications. However, generative applications such as neural rendering and inverse methods such as SVBRDF estimation and intrinsic image decomposition require additional input or output channels. Current solutions for channel expansion are often application specific and these solutions can be difficult to adapt to different diffusion models or new tasks. This paper introduces Teamwork: a flexible and efficient unified solution for jointly increasing the number of input and output channels as well as adapting a pretrained diffusion model to new tasks. Teamwork achieves channel expansion without altering the pretrained diffusion model architecture by coordinating and adapting multiple instances of the base diffusion model (\\ie, teammates). We employ a novel variation of Low Rank-Adaptation (LoRA) to jointly address both adaptation and coordination between the different teammates. Furthermore Teamwork supports dynamic (de)activation of teammates. We demonstrate the flexibility and efficiency of Teamwork on a variety of generative and inverse graphics tasks such as inpainting, single image SVBRDF estimation, intrinsic decomposition, neural shading, and intrinsic image synthesis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyuan","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Kaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LightCache: Memory-Efficient, Training-Free Acceleration for Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:54:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training-free acceleration has emerged as an advanced research area in video generation based on diffusion models. The redundancy of latents in diffusion model inference provides a natural entry point for acceleration. In this paper, we decompose the inference process into the encoding, denoising, and decoding stages, and observe that cache-based acceleration methods often lead to substantial memory surges in the latter two stages. To address this problem, we analyze the characteristics of inference across different stages and propose stage-specific strategies for reducing memory consumption: 1) Asynchronous Cache Swapping. 2) Feature chunk. 3) Slicing latents to decode. At the same time, we ensure that the time overhead introduced by these three strategies remains lower than the acceleration gains themselves. Compared with the baseline, our approach achieves faster inference speed and lower memory usage, while maintaining quality degradation within an acceptable range. The Code is available at https://github.com/NKUShaw/LightCache ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:17Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:47:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05284","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05284","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05284","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Unnati","familyName":"Kashyap","name":"Kashyap, Unnati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas J.","familyName":"Maccarone","name":"Maccarone, Thomas J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliot C.","familyName":"Pattie","name":"Pattie, Eliot C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mason","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Mason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Swati","familyName":"Ravi","name":"Ravi, Swati","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra J.","familyName":"Tetarenko","name":"Tetarenko, Alexandra J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pau Bosch","familyName":"Cabot","name":"Cabot, Pau Bosch","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Herman L.","familyName":"Marshall","name":"Marshall, Herman L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"First X-ray and radio polarimetry of the neutron star low-mass X-ray binary GX 17+2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:54:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We report the first polarimetric results of the neutron star (NS) low-mass X-ray binary (LMXB) Z-source GX 17+2 using the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE) and the Very Large Array (VLA). We find that the X-ray source was polarized at PD = 1.9 +/- 0.3 % (1-sigma errors) with a polarization angle of PA = 11 +/- 4 degree (1-sigma errors). Simultaneous Nuclear Spectroscopic Telescope Array (NuSTAR) observations show that the source was in the normal branch (NB) during our IXPE observations. The X-ray spectro-polarimetry results suggest a source geometry comprising an accretion disk component, a Comptonization component, and a reflection component. The VLA radio polarization study shows a PD = 2.2 +/- 0.2 % with a Faraday-corrected intrinsic polarization angle of 1 +/- 5 degree, which is an indication of the jet axis. Thus, we find the estimated X-ray PA from the source is consistent with the radio PA. We discuss the accretion geometry of the Z-source in light of our X-ray spectro-polarimetry and radio findings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 5 Figures, 6 Tables, Accepted for publication in APJ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05284","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05205","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05205","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05205","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Wagner-Carena","name":"Wagner-Carena, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aizhan","familyName":"Akhmetzhanova","name":"Akhmetzhanova, Aizhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sydney","familyName":"Erickson","name":"Erickson, Sydney","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Data-Driven Prism: Multi-View Source Separation with Diffusion Model Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:00:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A common challenge in the natural sciences is to disentangle distinct, unknown sources from observations. Examples of this source separation task include deblending galaxies in a crowded field, distinguishing the activity of individual neurons from overlapping signals, and separating seismic events from an ambient background. Traditional analyses often rely on simplified source models that fail to accurately reproduce the data. Recent advances have shown that diffusion models can directly learn complex prior distributions from noisy, incomplete data. In this work, we show that diffusion models can solve the source separation problem without explicit assumptions about the source. Our method relies only on multiple views, or the property that different sets of observations contain different linear transformations of the unknown sources. We show that our method succeeds even when no source is individually observed and the observations are noisy, incomplete, and vary in resolution. The learned diffusion models enable us to sample from the source priors, evaluate the probability of candidate sources, and draw from the joint posterior of the source distribution given an observation. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method on a range of synthetic problems as well as real-world galaxy observations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to main conference of NeurIPS 2025. Code available at https://github.com/swagnercarena/ddprism"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05205","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianglin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jianglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hailing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Representation Potentials of Foundation Models for Multimodal Alignment: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:48:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models learn highly transferable representations through large-scale pretraining on diverse data. An increasing body of research indicates that these representations exhibit a remarkable degree of similarity across architectures and modalities. In this survey, we investigate the representation potentials of foundation models, defined as the latent capacity of their learned representations to capture task-specific information within a single modality while also providing a transferable basis for alignment and unification across modalities. We begin by reviewing representative foundation models and the key metrics that make alignment measurable. We then synthesize empirical evidence of representation potentials from studies in vision, language, speech, multimodality, and neuroscience. The evidence suggests that foundation models often exhibit structural regularities and semantic consistencies in their representation spaces, positioning them as strong candidates for cross-modal transfer and alignment. We further analyze the key factors that foster representation potentials, discuss open questions, and highlight potential challenges."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronen","familyName":"Kamenetsky","name":"Kamenetsky, Ronen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Dorfman","name":"Dorfman, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Garibi","name":"Garibi, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roni","familyName":"Paiss","name":"Paiss, Roni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Or","familyName":"Patashnik","name":"Patashnik, Or","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Cohen-Or","name":"Cohen-Or, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAEdit: Token-level control for continuous image editing via Sparse AutoEncoder"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:51:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale text-to-image diffusion models have become the backbone of modern image editing, yet text prompts alone do not offer adequate control over the editing process. Two properties are especially desirable: disentanglement, where changing one attribute does not unintentionally alter others, and continuous control, where the strength of an edit can be smoothly adjusted. We introduce a method for disentangled and continuous editing through token-level manipulation of text embeddings. The edits are applied by manipulating the embeddings along carefully chosen directions, which control the strength of the target attribute. To identify such directions, we employ a Sparse Autoencoder (SAE), whose sparse latent space exposes semantically isolated dimensions. Our method operates directly on text embeddings without modifying the diffusion process, making it model agnostic and broadly applicable to various image synthesis backbones. Experiments show that it enables intuitive and efficient manipulations with continuous control across diverse attributes and domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page at: https://ronen94.github.io/SAEdit/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:05:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergio","familyName":"Rozada","name":"Rozada, Sergio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vimal K.","familyName":"B.","name":"B., Vimal K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Cavallo","name":"Cavallo, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio G.","familyName":"Marques","name":"Marques, Antonio G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Jamali-Rad","name":"Jamali-Rad, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elvin","familyName":"Isufi","name":"Isufi, Elvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graph-Aware Diffusion for Signal Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:11:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the problem of generating graph signals from unknown distributions defined over given graphs, relevant to domains such as recommender systems or sensor networks. Our approach builds on generative diffusion models, which are well established in vision and graph generation but remain underexplored for graph signals. Existing methods lack generality, either ignoring the graph structure in the forward process or designing graph-aware mechanisms tailored to specific domains. We adopt a forward process that incorporates the graph through the heat equation. Rather than relying on the standard formulation, we consider a time-warped coefficient to mitigate the exponential decay of the drift term, yielding a graph-aware generative diffusion model (GAD). We analyze its forward dynamics, proving convergence to a Gaussian Markov random field with covariance parametrized by the graph Laplacian, and interpret the backward dynamics as a sequence of graph-signal denoising problems. Finally, we demonstrate the advantages of GAD on synthetic data, real traffic speed measurements, and a temperature sensor network."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:03:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilay","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Nilay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priyansh","familyName":"Bhandari","name":"Bhandari, Priyansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Maragatham","name":"Maragatham, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Text and Video Generation: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:39:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-video (T2V) generation technology holds potential to transform multiple domains such as education, marketing, entertainment, and assistive technologies for individuals with visual or reading comprehension challenges, by creating coherent visual content from natural language prompts. From its inception, the field has advanced from adversarial models to diffusion-based models, yielding higher-fidelity, temporally consistent outputs. Yet challenges persist, such as alignment, long-range coherence, and computational efficiency. Addressing this evolving landscape, we present a comprehensive survey of text-to-video generative models, tracing their development from early GANs and VAEs to hybrid Diffusion-Transformer (DiT) architectures, detailing how these models work, what limitations they addressed in their predecessors, and why shifts toward new architectural paradigms were necessary to overcome challenges in quality, coherence, and control. We provide a systematic account of the datasets, which the surveyed text-to-video models were trained and evaluated on, and, to support reproducibility and assess the accessibility of training such models, we detail their training configurations, including their hardware specifications, GPU counts, batch sizes, learning rates, optimizers, epochs, and other key hyperparameters. Further, we outline the evaluation metrics commonly used for evaluating such models and present their performance across standard benchmarks, while also discussing the limitations of these metrics and the emerging shift toward more holistic, perception-aligned evaluation strategies. Finally, drawing from our analysis, we outline the current open challenges and propose a few promising future directions, laying out a perspective for future researchers to explore and build upon in advancing T2V research and applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:02:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04947","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04947","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04947","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bidirectional Mammogram View Translation with Column-Aware and Implicit 3D Conditional Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:48:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:42:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dual-view mammography, including craniocaudal (CC) and mediolateral oblique (MLO) projections, offers complementary anatomical views crucial for breast cancer diagnosis. However, in real-world clinical workflows, one view may be missing, corrupted, or degraded due to acquisition errors or compression artifacts, limiting the effectiveness of downstream analysis. View-to-view translation can help recover missing views and improve lesion alignment. Unlike natural images, this task in mammography is highly challenging due to large non-rigid deformations and severe tissue overlap in X-ray projections, which obscure pixel-level correspondences. In this paper, we propose Column-Aware and Implicit 3D Diffusion (CA3D-Diff), a novel bidirectional mammogram view translation framework based on conditional diffusion model. To address cross-view structural misalignment, we first design a column-aware cross-attention mechanism that leverages the geometric property that anatomically corresponding regions tend to lie in similar column positions across views. A Gaussian-decayed bias is applied to emphasize local column-wise correlations while suppressing distant mismatches. Furthermore, we introduce an implicit 3D structure reconstruction module that back-projects noisy 2D latents into a coarse 3D feature volume based on breast-view projection geometry. The reconstructed 3D structure is refined and injected into the denoising UNet to guide cross-view generation with enhanced anatomical awareness. Extensive experiments demonstrate that CA3D-Diff achieves superior performance in bidirectional tasks, outperforming state-of-the-art methods in visual fidelity and structural consistency. Furthermore, the synthesized views effectively improve single-view malignancy classification in screening settings, demonstrating the practical value of our method in real-world diagnostics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"BIBM2025 accept, 8 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04947","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:59:52Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:59:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:59:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zilin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Jackson","name":"Jackson, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Foerster","name":"Foerster, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimon","familyName":"Whiteson","name":"Whiteson, Shimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HyperVLA: Efficient Inference in Vision-Language-Action Models via Hypernetworks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:15:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Built upon language and vision foundation models with strong generalization ability and trained on large-scale robotic data, Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have recently emerged as a promising approach to learning generalist robotic policies. However, a key drawback of existing VLAs is their extremely high inference costs. In this paper, we propose HyperVLA to address this problem. Unlike existing monolithic VLAs that activate the whole model during both training and inference, HyperVLA uses a novel hypernetwork (HN)-based architecture that activates only a small task-specific policy during inference, while still retaining the high model capacity needed to accommodate diverse multi-task behaviors during training. Successfully training an HN-based VLA is nontrivial so HyperVLA contains several key algorithm design features that improve its performance, including properly utilizing the prior knowledge from existing vision foundation models, HN normalization, and an action generation strategy. Compared to monolithic VLAs, HyperVLA achieves a similar or even higher success rate for both zero-shot generalization and few-shot adaptation, while significantly reducing inference costs. Compared to OpenVLA, a state-of-the-art VLA model, HyperVLA reduces the number of activated parameters at test time by $90\\times$, and accelerates inference speed by $120\\times$. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/MasterXiong/HyperVLA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:57:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04819","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04819","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04819","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benlin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Benlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amita","familyName":"Kamath","name":"Kamath, Amita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madeleine","familyName":"Grunde-McLaughlin","name":"Grunde-McLaughlin, Madeleine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Winson","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Winson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjay","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Ranjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Representations inside the Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:01:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:35:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite interpretability work analyzing VIT encoders and transformer activations, we don't yet understand why Multimodal Language Models (MLMs) struggle on perception-heavy tasks. We offer an under-studied perspective by examining how popular MLMs (LLaVA-OneVision, Qwen2.5-VL, and Llama-3-LLaVA-NeXT) process their visual key-value tokens. We first study the flow of visual information through the language model, finding that image value tokens encode sufficient information to perform several perception-heavy tasks zero-shot: segmentation, semantic correspondence, temporal correspondence, and referring expression detection. We find that while the language model does augment the visual information received from the projection of input visual encodings-which we reveal correlates with overall MLM perception capability-it contains less visual information on several tasks than the equivalent visual encoder (SigLIP) that has not undergone MLM finetuning. Further, we find that the visual information corresponding to input-agnostic image key tokens in later layers of language models contains artifacts which reduce perception capability of the overall MLM. Next, we discuss controlling visual information in the language model, showing that adding a text prefix to the image input improves perception capabilities of visual representations. Finally, we reveal that if language models were able to better control their visual information, their perception would significantly improve; e.g., in 33.3% of Art Style questions in the BLINK benchmark, perception information present in the language model is not surfaced to the output! Our findings reveal insights into the role of key-value tokens in multimodal systems, paving the way for deeper mechanistic interpretability of MLMs and suggesting new directions for training their visual encoder and language model components."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04819","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:54:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:54:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:54:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":52,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language+action%22+OR+VLA+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22+OR+%22multimodal+model%22+OR+%22multimodal+models%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}